-- Q12991: Prospective Analysis and Forecasting [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The R-squared of 0.92 is too high, indicating the model likely contains specification errors","The model cannot be validated because 60 observations falls below the minimum regression threshold","A holdout sample would test whether the model generalizes beyond the estimation data","The high R-squared on the estimation sample guarantees strong out-of-sample predictive performance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AICPA AT-C Section 301, training and testing on the same dataset inflates apparent accuracy through overfitting. A holdout sample reserves data not used in estimation to evaluate predictive power on unseen observations, providing a more reliable accuracy measure.
Wrong (A): High R-squared does not inherently indicate errors; it may reflect genuinely strong explanatory variables.
Wrong (B): Sixty observations generally exceeds minimum sample requirements; the concern is lack of out-of-sample validation, not insufficient data.
Wrong (D): High in-sample R-squared does not guarantee out-of-sample performance; overfitting can produce misleading accuracy metrics.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12991;

-- Q12993: Prospective Analysis and Forecasting [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The model is not significant at the 5% level, so the predictors jointly add no explanatory power","The model is highly significant because R-squared exceeds the 0.70 benchmark threshold","The F-statistic confirms that each individual predictor variable is statistically significant","The p-value of 0.12 means the model explains exactly 12% of variance in operating expenses"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AICPA AT-C Section 301, the F-test evaluates whether the model as a whole explains significantly more variance than an intercept-only model. A p-value of 0.12 exceeds the 0.05 threshold, so the null hypothesis that all coefficients are zero cannot be rejected.
Wrong (B): R-squared alone does not determine statistical significance; the F-test p-value must fall below the chosen significance level.
Wrong (C): The F-statistic tests joint significance of all predictors, not individual predictor significance, which requires t-tests.
Wrong (D): The p-value represents the probability of observing the F-statistic under the null hypothesis, not the percentage of variance explained.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12993;

