Wrong (C): With 15% spare capacity and 40% revenue growth, some incremental capacity is needed but not two entirely new plants.
Wrong (D): Fixed assets can be included in percentage-of-sales analysis, though the method must account for capacity utilization.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12994;

-- Q12998: Prospective Analysis and Forecasting [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Judgmental adjustments are never appropriate because they introduce bias into quantitative models","The adjustment is appropriate for known material events outside historical patterns, but risks optimism bias","The statistical model should always be trusted over management judgment since it is mathematically derived","The adjustment is valid only if the CFO can prove the exact dollar impact will occur as estimated"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C Section 305, judgmental adjustments to statistical forecasts are appropriate when known structural changes materially affect demand in ways historical data cannot capture. However, such adjustments introduce the risk of cognitive bias, particularly optimism bias, because estimates rely on subjective judgment.
Wrong (A): Prohibiting all manual adjustments is overly restrictive; structural events like competitor closures warrant adjustment when properly documented.
Wrong (C): Statistical models cannot capture unprecedented events, so management judgment is sometimes necessary to supplement quantitative outputs.
Wrong (D): Requiring exact proof of dollar amounts is impractical for forward-looking estimates; reasonable documentation of rationale is sufficient.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12998;

-- Q13236: Financial Valuation Methods [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Produce a lower valuation because forward multiples are always lower than trailing multiples","Produce a higher valuation because the normalized earnings base far exceeds depressed trailing EPS","Produce an identical valuation because markets adjust the multiple to offset earnings differences","Be inappropriate because forward multiples require audited projected financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 820-10-35, forward P/E multiples apply to projected future earnings. With trailing EPS of $0.50, a 20x trailing multiple yields $10.00. Using forward EPS of $2.00, even a compressed forward multiple produces a substantially higher valuation because the normalized earnings base is four times larger.
